You’re almost there – the only thing that stops the OSM raster tiles from lining up with the rest of the map is the **tile size**.  
Classic OSM slippy-map tiles are **256 px**, while MapLibre assumes **512 px** for raster sources unless you tell it otherwise. Add `tileSize: 256` (and, optionally, the usual metadata) when you create the source, then add a raster layer that points at it.

```js
// 1️⃣ Add the source
map.addSource('osm-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: [
    'https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
  ],
  tileSize: 256,          // ← required for correct scale
  // optional but recommended:
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 19,
  attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'
});

// 2️⃣ Add a raster layer (insert it below the first symbol layer so you don’t cover labels)
const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id;
map.addLayer(
  {
    id: 'osm-basemap',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'osm-basemap',
    paint: {}
  },
  firstSymbol   // ← insert below labels; omit or set to null to append on top
);
```

**What this does**

| Piece | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| `tileSize: 256` | Aligns the raster tiles with the map’s zoom levels (otherwise the imagery appears at the wrong scale). |
| `minzoom` / `maxzoom` | Gives MapLibre the correct zoom range; OSM serves 0-19. |
| `attribution` | Required by OSM’s license and shows up in the map’s attribution control. |
| Inserting the layer **before** the first `symbol` layer | Prevents the raster from covering street-name labels and other symbols. |

With those fields in place the OSM raster tiles will load, display at the proper scale, and coexist nicely with any vector layers or labels you add later. No additional CORS configuration is needed – the OSM tile server already sends the proper `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header.

You’re almost there – the only thing you need to add is the **`tileSize`** (OSM tiles are 256 px, while MapLibre defaults to 512 px for raster sources).  
A minimal, working setup looks like this:

```js
// 1️⃣ Add the raster source
map.addSource('osm-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: ['https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
  tileSize: 256,          // ← required for correct zoom/scale
  // optional but recommended metadata
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 19,
  attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'
});

// 2️⃣ Add a raster layer that draws the source
map.addLayer({
  id: 'osm-basemap-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'osm-basemap',
  // Insert it below the first symbol layer so you don’t cover labels
  // (find the first symbol layer id programmatically if you’re not sure)
  // e.g. map.addLayer(..., firstSymbolLayerId);
});
```

**Why `tileSize: 256` matters**  
If you omit it, MapLibre assumes 512 px tiles, so the OSM imagery will appear at the wrong zoom level (labels and other vector layers will be mis-aligned) and may look blurry or disappear as you zoom.

**Other optional goodies**

| Property | Why you might want it |
|----------|-----------------------|
| `minzoom` / `maxzoom` | Limits requests to the zoom range the OSM server actually serves (0-19). |
| `attribution` | Shows the required “© OpenStreetMap contributors” text in the map’s attribution control. |
| Layer insertion order | Adding the raster layer **before** the first `symbol` layer (or using `map.addLayer(..., beforeId)`) prevents the raster from covering street-name labels and icons. |

With the source defined as above and a raster layer added, the OSM tiles will render correctly. No further CORS configuration is needed—`tile.openstreetmap.org` already sends the proper `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header.

You’re almost there – the only thing that stops the OSM tiles from lining up with the rest of the map is the **default raster tile size**.  
MapLibre assumes raster tiles are 512 px unless you tell it otherwise, and the public OSM slippy-map tiles are 256 px. That makes the imagery appear at the wrong zoom (it looks “zoomed out” and then disappears as you zoom in).

Add `tileSize: 256` (and, optionally, the usual metadata) when you create the source, then add a raster layer that points at it.

```js
// 1️⃣ Define the raster source
map.addSource('osm-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: [
    'https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
  ],
  tileSize: 256,          // ← required for OSM tiles
  // optional but recommended:
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 19,
  attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'
});

// 2️⃣ Add a raster layer (insert it below the first symbol layer so you don’t cover labels)
const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id;
map.addLayer(
  {
    id: 'osm-basemap-layer',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'osm-basemap',
    paint: {}
  },
  firstSymbol   // insert below labels; omit this argument to append on top
);
```

**What else you might need, depending on the rest of your style**

| Item | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **`attribution`** | Some hosts (including OSM) require you to display proper credit. |
| **Layer order** | Adding the raster layer after existing vector layers will hide them; inserting it before the first `symbol` layer (as shown) keeps street names and icons visible. |
| **CORS** | OSM tiles already send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, so no extra work is needed. |
| **`glyphs` / `sprite`** | If your style also includes text or icons, make sure the style’s root `glyphs` and `sprite` URLs are set; otherwise labels or icons will be missing, but that’s unrelated to the raster source itself. |

With `tileSize: 256` (and the layer added in the right place) the OSM raster tiles will render correctly alongside any vector layers you have.

